  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Twenty thousand tons of steel folded into the river. In August 1907, the majestic Quebec Bridge was supposed to shatter world records, not shatter into pieces.",
        "visual_query": "st lawrence river bridge archival",
        "visual_fallback": "rusted steel bridge ruins"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Weeks before the disaster, riveters heard terrifying groans echo through the girders. Massive steel chords on the main anchor arm were visibly buckling, bending three inches out of straight.",
        "visual_query": "historic construction site steel beams",
        "visual_fallback": "industrial metal framing"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Though warning signs mounted, communication breakdowns and delayed reactions meant the order to halt work arrived too late.",
        "visual_query": "historical engineering blueprints office",
        "visual_fallback": "vintage office desk"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The math was fundamentally flawed from day one. Designers severely underestimated the massive dead weight of the cantilever structure, loading an unsupported frame far past its breaking point.",
        "visual_query": "massive steel bridge construction",
        "visual_fallback": "complex metal architecture"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Eighty-six workers fell with the iron. That fatal miscalculation completely rewrote global engineering safety standards forever.",
        "visual_query": "quebec bridge memorial plaque",
        "visual_fallback": "historic industrial memorial"
      }
    ],
